{
  "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
  "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:13 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Lewis Liman",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case concerns the statute dealing exclusively with penalty claims brought by government agencies to punish conduct made unlawful by statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Liman. Mr. Liman, you -- you are typing this a penalty case. The government says yes the accrual is the ordinary rule, but discovery is the rule when there is fraud, and fraud is alleged here. So how does the Court decide whether to type this case a penalty case, as you urge, or a fraud case, as the government urged -- urges, when both captions fit?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Liman, I understand your argument, but I have a fundamental difficulty, okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. -- Mr. Liman, you acknowledge that a civil action could be brought beginning from the time when the injured plaintiff discovers the fraud, right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And never in a criminal case, right? Do you have a single case in which the discovery rule was -- was applied in a criminal case with respect to a penalty or a criminal sanction?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey B. Wall",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It doesn't matter how it's labeled. It doesn't matter whether we label it as an interpretation of the statute or an exception for cases of fraud or concealment. The result is the same."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And until 2004 I haven't found a single case in which the government ever tried to assert the discovery rule where what they were seeking was a civil penalty, not to try to make themselves whole where they are a victim, with one exception, a case called Maillard in the 19th century where they did make that assertion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All I'm asking you for is one case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But, Mr. Wall, I'll go even further than Justice Ginsburg. I mean this case actually seems to me a good example when Mr. Liman said there's no natural starting point and Justice Kennedy and Justice Alito referred to just -- this is a -- this is a decision about enforcement priorities. The government had decided not to go after market timers. And it changed its decision when a State attorney general decided to do it, and it embarrassed them that they had made that enforcement priority decision, and then the government made a different enforcement priority decision. But that's not the kind of situation that the discovery rule was intended to operate on, is it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jeffrey B. Wall",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are 25 reported cases"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, if all that's true, and this is a point I want you to -- I'm not sure I am right about this point, but remember your banking case now, we're sounding like that, I thought -- doesn't the doctrine of fraudulent concealment still apply? That is, if the defendant, in fact, takes any affirmative action to hide what's going on, the statute will be tolled. Is that right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the chance of this somehow escaping notice without fraudulent concealment, which would allow the government to extend the toll strikes me as small, but am I right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, perhaps I've missed something. I -- I came in here thinking that both parties were willing to concede for purposes of this case that there was a fraudulent concealment. Is that -- is that wrong?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Wall, why is it that you don't you have any cases? I mean, you said way back when: This didn't come up, this is a modern problem. So explain to me why this is a modern problem. This is obviously an old statute. Are you saying that this statute has not been used very -- was not used very much until very, very recently?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0007",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There are very few cases that deal with this statute at all and, obviously, in this context because the Commission's only had the ability to bring civil penalties for about 20 years. But I think that is not a problem unknown to the law. Again and again, facing garden variety limitations provisions written just like this one, this Court applied the fraud discovery rule."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But what you're running up against is a skepticism, that -- you know, the government, which has not -- asserted this power for 200 years, is now coming in and saying we want it. And the question is why hasn't the government asserted this power previously?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Would you agree that when we're talking about the interests in repose that the one plaintiff we should be particularly concerned about is the government?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0009",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes, our argument would absolutely be different in a criminal context."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b3c6211ead19bb95bf3eaa1b205197ba19730b8c8b1c775abb0468fa85221835",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b3c6211ead19-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Second, the concession that you just heard a moment ago, that the statute would not apply as the government says it should apply if this was deemed to be a criminal penalty, we submit under this Court's reasoning in Clark v. Martinez, it just gave away the store in the government's case because if it is possible -- as the government has now admitted it's possible -- and I don't want to get into all of the permutations of Hudson -- but if it is possible that the label of civil penalty does not -- is not dispositive as to whether a penalty is civil or criminal, then, as the Court held in Clark v. Martinez, the lowest common denominator applies."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2012-11-1274",
      "confidence": 0.0,
